  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/4906a1de7cc1eb78bbff845fe6b03b024b47c46e84c801609e8da40a5d37c864.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $2$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{\u00a0________\u00a0}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*",
  "worked_solution": "The $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(2)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(2)=2.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,2]$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/455863da8f126505f018a356f5f89bbbea81f1e1e90e803a51390bed22514a9a.png)",
